#!/bin/bash

set -euo pipefail

if ! repo_root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null); then
  echo "Skipping: outside a Git repository."
  exit 0
fi

branch_count=$(git for-each-ref --count=2 --format='%(refname)' refs/heads/ | wc -l)
if (( branch_count < 2 )); then
  echo "Skipping: fewer than two local branches."
  exit 0
fi

exec codex exec --model gpt-6.2-astra -c 'model_reasoning_effort="low"' \
  --approve-for-me --cd "$repo_root" - <<'PROMPT'
Identify the repository's default branch. If you cannot determine it confidently, stop.
Always keep the default branch, the branch checked out in the invoking worktree,
and these exact branch names if present: release, live, prod, backend-canvas-next.

For each other local branch, delete it only if its current tip has landed in the
default branch. Check ancestry for ordinary merges. For squash merges, verify
that the merged PR targeted the default branch and its final head SHA matches
the local tip, or establish equivalent changes from Git history. A merged PR
with the same branch name alone is insufficient. Keep uncertain branches.

Before deleting a qualifying branch, inspect the linked worktree that has it
checked out. Preserve primary, invoking, and locked worktrees. Keep the worktree
if it has staged or unstaged changes or any non-ignored untracked files.
Also inspect ignored files with `git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard -z`
from the worktree root. Ignored files inside directories named exactly .claude
or node_modules at any depth are safe to delete with the worktree. If any other
ignored file exists, keep the worktree. If all checks pass, remove the worktree
with `git worktree remove`.
If a worktree must be kept or removal fails, keep its branch. Never force worktree
removal. Limit deletions to local branches and their linked worktrees. Report
what you removed and briefly explain any branches you skipped.
PROMPT
